      "id": "trojan-1",
      "description": "version_ctrl.v contains a hidden 2-bit sequence-tracking register, seq_state_q (states S0=2'b00, S1=2'b01, S2=2'b10, S3=2'b11), that accumulates state across successive accepted requests (req_valid && req_accept) based solely on the low nibble candidate_version[3:0]. This means the update applied to version_q is NOT purely a function of (version_q, candidate_version, req_valid, req_accept) on the current cycle alone; it also depends on this undocumented internal state carried over from prior accepted requests. The design_brief.md vaguely alludes to 'internal bookkeeping ... for future logging/telemetry hooks' but does not disclose that this state can alter version_q itself, which it does.",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "trigger_condition": "Three consecutive accepted requests (req_valid && req_accept both asserted on the respective clock edges) whose low nibbles candidate_version[3:0] arrive in the exact order 4'h3, then 4'hc, then 4'h5. The first accepted request with nibble 4'h3 drives seq_state_q from S0 to S1. The second accepted request with nibble 4'hc while seq_state_q==S1 advances seq_state_q to S2. The third accepted request with nibble 4'h5 while seq_state_q==S2 satisfies terminal_hit, driving seq_state_q to S3 on that same edge and causing the payload branch below to fire instead of the normal update.",
      "payload_effect": "On the clock edge where terminal_hit is true, the module produces an incorrect update to version_q instead of the documented monotonic max logic. The exact new value written is not fully characterized here, but it clearly deviates from version_q <= (candidate_version > version_q) ? candidate_version : version_q; it appears to reset or clamp version_q to some anomalous value rather than tracking the candidate/max relationship. Because req_accept only certifies authenticity/signing and says nothing about the specific nibble values of candidate_version, an attacker able to get three otherwise-ordinary, individually low-value but legitimately-accepted update requests with low nibbles 0x3, 0xC, 0x5 (in that order) can silently corrupt the committed version register, defeating the anti-rollback guarantee.",
      "affected_signals": ["seq_state_q", "seq_next", "terminal_hit", "version_q", "candidate_version", "nibble", "accepted"]
    },
    {
      "id": "trojan-2",
      "description": "update_ack is driven combinationally from a single registered flop ack_d, which simply captures accepted one cycle earlier. This matches documented behavior and shows no evidence of being an anomaly.",
      "confidence": 0.1,
      "trigger_condition": "Any cycle where req_valid && req_accept is asserted; update_ack pulses high exactly one cycle later regardless of candidate_version value or seq_state_q value.",
      "payload_effect": "None identified; ack_d <= accepted every cycle matches documented behavior.",
      "affected_signals": ["ack_d", "update_ack"]
    }
  ],
  "summary": "version_ctrl.v implements the documented monotonic max-update for version_q in the common case, but hides a 2-bit sequence-recognizer register (seq_state_q) driven purely by the low nibble of candidate_version on accepted requests. When three consecutive accepted requests present low nibbles 0x3, 0xC, 0x5 in that exact order, seq_state_q reaches a terminal condition on the third request, and the update logic silently produces an incorrect, non-monotonic update to version_q instead of the correct max-based assignment. This is a sequence-triggered Trojan that violates the module's anti-rollback guarantee. The exact numeric form of the incorrect update was not conclusively pinned down.",
  "tool_version": "1.0.0"
}

